o sees a doctor or pays for care.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"D000197","score":0.08364698844899629},"overlapCount":48},"meaning":{"percentile":97.44439185991482,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4735445527585572}},{"billId":"119-hr-685","bioguideId":"K000376","cycle":2026,"score":0.06359315168086399,"matches":[{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":78500,"donorShare":0.0793265268815368,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.037459748805170154},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":49000,"donorShare":0.04951592123815673,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.02613340287569383}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4735445527585572},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-685","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"685","title":"SAVE Moms and Babies Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-23","latestActionDate":"2025-01-23","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Drug safety, medical device, and laboratory regulation","Health information and medical records","Health personnel","Health technology, devices, supplies","Marketing and advertising","Prescription drugs"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000566","cosponsorCount":62,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/685","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly prohibits approval of new abortion drugs and imposes significant new regulatory restrictions on already-approved drugs in this category."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new certification requirements for prescribing practitioners and mandates they have surgical intervention capability, changing practice obligations."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would prohibit the FDA from approving any new drugs intended to terminate pregnancy. For drugs already approved for this purpose, it would impose new restrictions: requiring in-person dispensing by a specially certified practitioner, prohibiting use after 70 days of gestation, and mandating that the practitioner be able to provide surgical intervention. The bill would also prevent practitioners from both prescribing and dispensing these medications.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who fills a prescription. Anyone who takes a medicine.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.11251216543663692},"overlapCount":62},"meaning":{"percentile":96.95535573434296,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4735445527585572}},{"billId":"119-hr-7","bioguideId":"K000376","cycle":2026,"score":0.05560881670863821,"matches":[{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":78500,"donorShare":0.0793265268815368,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.026442175627178936},{"industry":"insurance","donorAmount":68000,"donorShare":0.06871597233050322,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.025600068123128653},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","donorAmount":12000,"donorShare":0.012126348058324098,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.003566572958330617}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4735445527585572},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-7","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"7","title":"No Taxpayer Funding for Abortion and Abortion Insurance Full Disclosure Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committees on the Judiciary, and Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Appropriations","Assault and harassment offenses","Comprehensive health care","Consumer affairs","Crimes against women","Employee benefits and pensions","Government buildings, facilities, and property","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health facilities and institutions","Health programs administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000522","cosponsorCount":109,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts what health insurance plans may cover and how federal subsidies interact with abortion coverage in qualified health plans."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill prohibits federal health care facilities and federal employees from providing abortion services, affecting facilities that currently receive federal funds."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Organizations focused on reproductive health policy and abortion access or restrictions have a direct stake in the regulatory framework this bill establishes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make permanent the restrictions on federal funding for abortion services and health insurance coverage that includes abortion. Currently, these restrictions are renewed each year through appropriations bills; the bill would apply them across all federal programs and funds. It would also prohibit qualified health plans offered through insurance marketplaces from covering abortion. The bill includes exceptions for cases of rape, incest, or when the pregnant person's life is in danger.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys health insurance. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.061260165720508956},"overlapCount":107},"meaning":{"percentile":95.64600094652154,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4735445527585572}},{"billId":"119-hjres-88","bioguideId":"K000376","cycle":2026,"score":0.012605478189938913,"matches":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":31000,"donorShare":0.031326399150670585,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.007561544622575658},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":17000,"donorShare":0.017178993082625806,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.003554274430888098},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":4500,"donorShare":0.004547380521871537,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0014896591364751586}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4735445527585572},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-88","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"88","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine Pollution Control Standards; Advanced Clean Cars II; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-16.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Federal preemption","Hybrid, electric, and advanced technology vehicles","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000302","cosponsorCount":41,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/88","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Automotive manufacturers face different emission and zero-emission vehicle requirements depending on whether California's waiver stands or is revoked."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Zero-emission vehicle mandates directly affect demand for gasoline and diesel fuel in California's large vehicle market."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Electric vehicle adoption rates influence demand for charging infrastructure and electricity from renewable sources."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The pace of electric vehicle deployment affects electricity demand and grid infrastructure investment needs."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would cancel a federal waiver that allows California to enforce its Advanced Clean Cars II regulations. Those regulations set stricter standards for vehicle emissions and require automakers to sell more zero-emission vehicles in California. If the resolution passes, California would lose the authority to enforce these stricter rules, reverting to federal emission standards.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"L000566","score":0.035788137691442914},"overlapCount":41},"meaning":{"percentile":56.17605300520587,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4735445527585572}},{"billId":"119-hjres-89","bioguideId":"K000376","cycle":2026,"score":0.01109897690338275,"matches":[{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":23000,"donorShare":0.02324216711178785,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.005164926024841744},{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":12050,"donorShare":0.012176874508567114,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.003672390724805955},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":4500,"donorShare":0.004547380521871537,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0012992515776775822},{"industry":"agriculture","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.005052645024301707,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.000962408576057468}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.4735445527585572},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-89","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"89","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine and Nonroad Engine Pollution Control Standards; The \"Omnibus\" Low NOX Regulation; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-17.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Federal preemption","Hybrid, electric, and advanced technology vehicles","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"O000019","cosponsorCount":32,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/89","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects emissions compliance obligations for manufacturers and operators of heavy-duty trucks, buses, and diesel-powered vehicles and equipment."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Vehicle and engine manufacturers would face different regulatory requirements depending on whether California's stricter emissions standards remain in effect or are preempted by federal rules."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Construction firms use off-road diesel equipment covered by the waiver, and the bill would affect compliance costs and equipment specifications for these machines."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Agricultural operations use off-road diesel engines and heavy-duty vehicles subject to the emissions standards that would be affected by revoking the waiver."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would cancel a waiver that allows California to enforce stricter emissions standards than federal rules for heavy-duty trucks, buses, and off-road diesel equipment starting with 2024 models. The waiver, granted by the Environmental Protection Agency under the Clean Air Act, permits California to set its own limits on nitrogen oxide pollution from these vehicles and engines. If passed, the resolution would restore federal preemption and prevent California from enforcing these particular state-level standards.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000806","score":0.05252904442813575},"overlapCount":32},"meaning":{"percentile":51.30146710837672,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.4735445527585572}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b759595d68f3a8d502","billId":"119-hr-7008","date":"2026-07-22T16:51:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll280.xml"},{"id":"2afa499bcaceefdcecfa862a","billId":"119-hr-8800","date":"2026-07-22T16:37:00-04:00","q